<div class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Core concepts</p>
  <h1>Reactivity</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SUI's reactivity is three small primitives: a <strong>signal</strong> holds a
    value, a <strong>derived</strong> computes one from others, and an
    <strong>effect</strong> runs a side-effect when its inputs change. No
    proxies, no dependency arrays — you read the graph.
  </p>

  <h2>Signals</h2>
  <p>
    A signal is a readable, writable value. Call it to read; call
    <code>.set()</code> to write. Reading a signal inside a derived or effect
    subscribes to it automatically.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.signal}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Derived</h2>
  <p>
    A derived value recomputes from the signals it reads. It's lazy and cached —
    it only recomputes when you read it <em>and</em> an input actually changed.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.derived}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Effects</h2>
  <p>
    An effect runs immediately, then re-runs whenever a signal it read changes.
    Use it for the edges of your app — logging, the DOM, network, storage.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.effect}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>In a component</h2>
  <p>
    Templates bind to signals directly. When the signal changes, only the exact
    DOM that read it updates — no virtual DOM, no re-render of the component.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.template}></sui-codeblock>
  <p class="callout">
    <strong>The mental model:</strong> a text binding is itself an effect the
    compiler wrote for you. Change the signal, and that one text node updates —
    nothing else re-runs. That's the whole runtime.
  </p>

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/getting-started">← Getting started</a>
    <a class="btn" href="/data">The data seam →</a>
  </div>
</div>
